ht on  in this one and soon enough we see all  minor pieces exchanged in this one and  we're working now with just rooks and  queens some structural defect on the  white side but it's never it shouldn't  really be enough to turn it into  advantage but what we end up having is a  material plus on Carlsen side soon  enough okay a lot of dancing at this  point soon enough we end up having just  the rooks on the board right here Anand  gives up upon in order to have a very  active rook h5 is thrown in giving up  another pawn to shatter the structure  and now slowly but surely white well  black is going to now improve the King  position black gives up not only the  b-pawn but also the sea Pond and this is  this is a big moment coming up here in  the match after the roof gives a check  King seif king f4 means that it's now  the sea pond will be given up light has  connected past ponds but black has a  very very dangerous pond in that of the  F pond which simply at this point can  not be stopped and soon enough we end up  having after rook g1 Anand resigning for  now the second time and Carlsen going up  by two points and again Anand later  describing this one as a very heavy blow  in the match Game seven well not having  the white pieces we once again have the  Roy Lopes but now with some twist  grabbing on c6  it's turned out to be a very positional  battle and no imbalance with regard to  the minor pieces but rather just the  pawn structure some activity eventually  ensues on the h-file with it now being  peeled open that  is going to be an indication more pieces  will be exchanged the rooks in  particular and sure enough we have both  sets of rooks coming off and this one at  the end of it we end up having the  Queen's and the Knights and not really  much to make of this position similar to  games 1 &amp; 2 we end up having a little  back-and-forth with the Knights and the  Queen here and this one here ending as a  draw so not at this point is certainly  running out of